  "response": "**Setting up the calculation (Bayes' theorem):**\n\n- Prevalence: P(D+) = 1/500 = 0.002, so P(D−) = 0.998\n- Sensitivity: P(T+|D+) = 0.98\n- Specificity: P(T+|D−) = 1 − 0.95 = 0.05 (false positive rate)\n\n**True positives:** 0.002 × 0.98 = 0.00196\n**False positives:** 0.998 × 0.05 = 0.04990\n\n**Total positives:** 0.00196 + 0.04990 = 0.05186\n\n**Posterior probability:**\nP(D+|T+) = 0.00196 / 0.05186 ≈ 0.037794 → **3.78%**\n\nSo even with a positive test result, there's only about a 3.78% chance the person actually has the condition — because the condition is so rare that false positives from the 5% of the large healthy population vastly outnumber the true positives from the tiny affected population.\n\nFINAL ANSWER: 3.78",
